  lessons: [
  {t:"The CPA Credential & the Evolution Model", d:"Three core sections plus one discipline", len:"16 min",
   script:"The Certified Public Accountant credential is issued by a state board of accountancy, but the exam behind it is national. Since January of 2024 the Uniform C P A Examination has followed the C P A Evolution model. Every candidate sits three core sections: Auditing and Attestation, Financial Accounting and Reporting, and Taxation and Regulation. Then each candidate picks one discipline section from three choices. The core sections test what every newly licensed accountant is expected to know. The discipline lets you go deep in the area you intend to practice in. All four sections run four hours and all four are scored the same way. Choosing a discipline does not restrict your license in any way."},
  {t:"AUD — Auditing & Attestation", d:"78 multiple choice + 7 simulations, 4 hours", len:"55 min",
   script:"Auditing and Attestation is the section candidates underestimate. It carries seventy-eight multiple choice questions and seven task based simulations across four hours. The blueprint covers four areas: ethics, professional responsibilities and general principles; assessing risk and developing a planned response; performing further procedures and obtaining evidence; and forming conclusions and reporting. Independence is the spine of the whole section. Independence is required for attest work. It is not required for tax preparation, for bookkeeping, or for most consulting engagements. Expect heavy testing on internal control, on sampling, and on the exact wording of a modified audit report. Much of A U D is judgment rather than computation."},
  {t:"FAR — Financial Accounting & Reporting", d:"50 multiple choice + 7 simulations, 4 hours", len:"60 min",
   script:"Financial Accounting and Reporting has the smallest question count and the largest reputation. Fifty multiple choice questions and seven task based simulations, again in four hours. The blueprint splits three ways: the financial reporting framework, select balance sheet accounts, and select transactions. When the exam was redesigned, the heaviest state and local government material and several advanced technical topics moved out to the Business Analysis and Reporting discipline. So F A R is narrower than the version your colleagues sat, but it is not easier. Practice assembling a complete set of financial statements, and drill leases, revenue recognition, bonds and deferred taxes until the journal entries arrive without hesitation."},
  {t:"REG — Taxation & Regulation", d:"72 multiple choice + 8 simulations, 4 hours", len:"55 min",
   script:"Taxation and Regulation carries seventy-two multiple choice questions and eight task based simulations. Roughly three quarters of the section is federal taxation: individual taxation, entity taxation, and property transactions. The remainder is ethics and professional responsibility in tax practice, plus business law. Circular two thirty governs practice before the Internal Revenue Service, and it applies to certified public accountants, to attorneys, to enrolled agents and to enrolled actuaries alike. Basis is the concept that repays study the most. If you can track basis through a contribution, a distribution and a sale, for a partnership and for an S corporation, you have already secured a large share of the available points."},
  {t:"Choosing Your Discipline: BAR, ISC or TCP", d:"One of three, and the choice is yours", len:"40 min",
   script:"After the three core sections you choose one discipline, and the choice is entirely yours. Business Analysis and Reporting carries fifty multiple choice questions and seven simulations, and picks up advanced financial reporting, state and local government accounting, and financial management. Information Systems and Controls carries eighty-two multiple choice questions and six simulations, covering information technology audit, system and organization controls engagements, and data management. Tax Compliance and Planning carries sixty-eight multiple choice questions and seven simulations, going deeper into individual and entity tax planning. Information Systems and Controls is the one section where multiple choice questions count for sixty percent of your score rather than fifty."},
  {t:"Scoring, the Credit Window & Scheduling", d:"Scaled 75, the 30-month rule, continuous testing", len:"20 min",
   script:"Every section is scored on a scale from zero to ninety-nine, and seventy-five is passing. That number is not a percentage correct. It is a scaled score that accounts for the difficulty of the questions you saw, so you cannot study toward a fixed count of right answers. Outside Information Systems and Controls, multiple choice questions and simulations each contribute half of your score. Once you pass your first section, the clock starts on your credit window. The old rule was eighteen months. The amended model rule sets thirty months, measured from the date your score is released, and nearly every board has now adopted it. Indiana publishes an even longer thirty-six month window."},
  {t:"State Board Licensure & the 150-Hour Rule", d:"State-specific — updates automatically per state", len:"22 min", stateSpecific:true,
   script:null},
  ],

